Benchmark log of the optimisation work
======================================

One directory per machine, one directory per measured step inside it:

  bench/log/MACHINE/SUMMARY.md     every step on that machine in one table:
                                   totals, speed-up against the previous
                                   step and against the baseline
  bench/log/MACHINE/NN-NAME/
    EXPLANATION.md                 what was measured, under which
                                   conditions, what the numbers say
    raw/                           everything the run printed, unedited

Each step is measured with bench/campaign.sh STEP, which records:

  raw/conditions.txt     commit, uncommitted changes, CPU, governor,
                         power, compiler, configure options, load
  raw/uncommitted.patch  the exact source measured, when it was not
                         yet committed (the step's own commit follows)
  raw/campaign.log       progress and time of each stage
  raw/build.log, check.log, build-bench.log
  raw/bench.log, bench.csv, bench.txt
                         make bench: the codec as shipped
                         (--enable-release), REPEAT runs per file
  raw/archives.sha256    checksum of the archive of every corpus file;
                         an optimisation that keeps the format must keep
                         these identical
  raw/bench-compare.log, variants.csv, variants.txt
                         make bench-compare: -O0 to -O3, -march=native,
                         LTO, PGO
  raw/profile.log, profile/
                         make profile: gprof, perf stat/record, callgrind,
                         cachegrind on silesia/dickens

perf.data, callgrind.out, cachegrind.out and the profiled archive are
binary and large; they stay on the machine that made them and are not
committed.

Measurements are pinned to one core with taskset.  Times from
different machines are never compared with each other, only steps on
the same machine.

Machines
--------

  debian       AMD Ryzen 7 5700U (Zen 2), 8 cores / 16 threads, 30 GB,
               AVX2 and BMI2, no AVX-512; GCC 14.2
  hppavillion  Intel Core i3-1115G4 (Tiger Lake), 2 cores / 4 threads,
               7.4 GB, AVX-512BW; GCC 16.2

Steps
-----

  00-baseline           dev at b5f6d00 plus the measuring scripts
  01-block-io           #11
  02-inline-bitio       #12
  03-no-memmove         #14
  04-compare-8-bytes    #15
  05-avx512bw           #16, measured on hppavillion
  06-avx2               AVX2 match compare, measured on debian
  07-lto-pgo            #13
